{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"7f78eac923e14558","name":"card_event_desert_raid","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Britain/BrothersInArms/events/card_event_desert_raid","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"DESERT RAID","text":"All units deal 1 combat damage until your next turn.","flavorText":"The Raid on Sidi Haneish Airfield was a military operation carried out by the British Special Air Service (SAS) during WWII, specifically on the night of December 26-27, 1942. The objective of the raid was to disrupt enemy air operations and destroy aircraft at the airfield, which was located in Libya and was being used by German and Italian forces. The operation was conducted by a force consisting of the Long Range Desert Group (LRDG) and the Special Air Service (SAS). The LRDG provided the vehicles and navigation expertise and the SAS carried out the actual raid on the airfield. The raid was carried out with 18 jeeps that drove through the night in formation without headlights, each carrying 3-4 British or French commandos and mounted Vickers K machine guns. On arrival the SAS stormed the airfield in V formation and used their mounted machine guns to fire on and destroy the parked German aircraft on the airfield. The Germans fired back using machine guns and anti-aircraft weapons but were only able to disable one jeep and one SAS was shot and killed. The raid was a success, with the SAS destroying at least 30 enemy aircraft, damaging several others, and causing significant damage to the airfield infrastructure. Afterwards the raiders escaped through the desert and were able to return safely to Allied lines. The Raid on Sidi Haneish Airfield was significant because it demonstrated the effectiveness of special operations forces in disrupting enemy operations and causing damage with minimal casualties. It also highlighted the importance of intelligence gathering and navigation expertise in planning and executing successful raids.","faction":"Brita